  <p>Or fill in the template above and download a finished PDF: the totals, tax and invoice number are handled for you, and your details are remembered for next time.</p>

  <h2>What to put on a freelance invoice</h2>
  <table class="tbl">
    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="strong">Your name and contact details</td><td>Sam Rivera, Design &amp; Illustration, sam@example.com</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="strong">Client name and address</td><td>Northwind Ltd, 12 Market Street</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="strong">Invoice number</td><td>INV-014 (one continuous sequence)</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="strong">Invoice date and due date</td><td>2 March 2026, due 16 March 2026</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="strong">The work, line by line</td><td>Landing page design, 12 hours at 65.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="strong">Tax, if you are registered</td><td>VAT, GST or HST line with your registration number</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="strong">Total and how to pay</td><td>Bank details or payment link, and the reference to quote</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Hourly, project or retainer: how to write the lines</h2>
  <div class="example">
    <h3>Hourly</h3>
    <p>One line per task. Quantity = hours, rate = hourly rate. "Copywriting: product pages, 8 hours x 60.00 = 480.00".</p>
  </div>
  <div class="example">
    <h3>Fixed-price project</h3>
    <p>One line per deliverable or milestone. "Milestone 2 of 3: homepage build = 1,200.00". Say which milestone it is so the client can match it to the quote.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="example">
    <h3>Monthly retainer</h3>
    <p>One line for the month, extra work on separate lines. "Retainer, April 2026 = 1,500.00" plus "Additional hours beyond retainer, 3 x 70.00 = 210.00".</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Payment terms that get freelancers paid</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Write an actual due date ("due 16 March 2026"), not only "Net 14".</li>
    <li>Put your bank details or payment link on the invoice itself, not in a separate email.</li>
    <li>Ask for a deposit on new or large projects and show it as a credit on the final invoice.</li>
    <li>State your late-payment terms before the work starts. See <a href="/how-to-charge-late-payment-fee">how to charge a late payment fee</a>.</li>
  </ul>

